./audits/workspace_findings.json:383:    "issue": "Memory Corruption and Undefined Behavior via Unpadded In-Place JSON Deserialization <br><br>The `pattern_tracker` and `workflow_tracker` utilize `unsafe { simd_json::from_str(&mut agent_sequence_json) }` on raw string values retrieved directly from SQLite. `simd-json` requires a padded buffer (`simd_json::PADDING_SIZE` of 32 or 64 bytes) to safely perform vectorized SIMD operations. Passing standard `String` structures without padding forces SIMD registers to read past allocated bounds, causing arbitrary memory access, undefined behavior, or segmentation faults during runtime.",
./audits/workspace_findings.json:798:    "recommendation": "`simd_json` relies on hardware-level SIMD instructions that strictly require input buffers to have trailing padding of size `simd_json::PADDING` (typically 32 or 64 bytes) to prevent out-of-bounds reads/writes. The `transact` implementations clone a raw incoming D-Bus `String` and immediately invoke `unsafe { simd_json::from_str(&mut cloned_str) }`. Since standard Rust `String` allocations from the D-Bus carrier are not padded, this is directly exploitable by sending specifically sized messages to trigger undefined behavior, memory corruption, or segment faults. To remediate, extract the string as a `Vec<u8>`, push the required padding bytes, and parse it safely, or utilize the safe `serde_json::from_str` parser."
